a dairy product traditionally made from sheep's milk, but now it is more often made industrially from cow's milk. It is popular in the northern regions of Spain (Asturias, Cantabria, Basque Country, Navarre, Aragon, Castile and Leon, and La Rioja). In Latin America it is popular in Colombia, Venezuela, and in the Central American countries of El Salvador, Honduras, Nicaragua, and in the northern region of Costa Rica."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"dish-cuajada"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cuajada"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's from","links":[["Spanish cuisine","cuisine/spanish-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Ingredients","count":4,"links":[["Ewe's milk","ingredient/ewe-s-milk"],["Milk","ingredient/milk"],["Raw milk","ingredient/raw-milk"],["Rennet","ingredient/rennet"]]},{"h":"Cook it","rows":[{"name":"Spain on a Fork","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"spainonafork.com","note":"Albert Bevia's video channel and site of traditional and everyday Spanish recipes.","url":"https://spainonafork.com/welcome2/","suggested":true,"kind":"youtube"}]},{"h":"More from Spanish cuisine","links":[["Andrajos","dish/andrajos"],["Bolinhos de bacalhau","dish/bolinhos-de-bacalhau"],["Buñuelo","dish/bunuelo"],["Caldo galego","dish/caldo-galego"],["Chistorra","dish/chistorra"],["Churro","dish/churro"],["Cocido","dish/cocido"],["Embutido","dish/embutido"],["Gazpacho","dish/gazpacho"],["Hojuela","dish/hojuela"],["Jamón","dish/jamon"],["Migas","dish/migas"],["Mojo (sauce)","dish/mojo-sauce"],["Patatas a lo pobre","dish/patatas-a-lo-pobre"],["Patatas bravas","dish/patatas-bravas"],["Picatostes","dish/picatostes"],["Pincho","dish/pincho"],["Pottage","dish/pottage"],["Salchichón","dish/salchichon"],["Salmorejo","dish/salmorejo"],["Sobrassada","dish/sobrassada"],["Soldaditos de Pavía","dish/soldaditos-de-pavia"],["Sopa de gato","dish/sopa-de-gato"],["Spanish omelette","dish/spanish-omelette"],["Spanish restaurant","dish/spanish-restaurant"],["Stuffed peppers","dish/stuffed-peppers"],["Tapas","dish/tapas"],["Turrón","dish/turron"]]}],"img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/1/18/Cuajada_cropped_-_juantigues.jpg/500px-Cuajada_cropped_-_juantigues.jpg","w":500,"h":444,"by":"Juan Mejuto","lic":"CC BY-SA 2.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/2.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Cuajada_cropped_-_juantigues.jpg"}},"dish/doogh":{"t":"dish","r":"dish/doogh","n":"Doogh","c":"#ede6da","crumb":[["Dishes","dishes"]],"sub":"Dish from Iranian cuisine","dot":"#f0b340","intro":[["Doogh is a cold and savoury Persian drink. It is made with fermented milk. Unlike ayran, a yoghurt drink of Turkish origin, doogh is not diluted yogurt. According to the Iranian Ministry of Food Standards, doogh \"is a drink resulting from lactic fermentation of milk whose dry matter is standardized by diluting yogurt (after fermentation) or buttermilk (before fermentation).\""]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"dish-doogh"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Doogh"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's from","links":[["Iranian cuisine","cuisine/iranian-cuisine"]]},{"h":"Ingredients","count":2,"links":[["Buttermilk","ingredient/buttermilk"],["Yogurt","ingredient/yogurt"]]},{"h":"Cook it","rows":[{"name":"Unicorns in the Kitchen","color":"#55b8ad","shape":"site","sub":"unicornsinthekitchen.com","note":"Iranian-born Shadi HasanzadeNemati's blog with a large Persian recipe section.","url":"https://www.unicornsinthekitchen.com/persian-recipes","suggested":true,"kind":"blog"}]},{"h":"More from Iranian cuisine","links":[["Akuri","dish/akuri"],["Cabbage roll","dish/cabbage-roll"],["Chelow kabab","dish/chelow-kabab"],["Falooda","dish/falooda"],["Jujeh kabab","dish/jujeh-kabab"],["Kabab torsh","dish/kabab-torsh"],["Keşkek","dish/keskek"],["Khash (dish)","dish/khash-dish"],["Khoresh karafs","dish/khoresh-karafs"],["Lighvan cheese","dish/lighvan-cheese"],["Nisholda","dish/nisholda"],["Pilaf","dish/pilaf"],["Scorched rice","dish/scorched-rice"],["Sheermal","dish/sheermal"],["Shirazi salad","dish/shirazi-salad"],["Shirin polo","dish/shirin-polo"],["Sholezard","dish/sholezard"]]}],"img":{"src":"/labs/cuisines/img/thumb/c/c8/%D0%A2%D0%90%D0%9D_%28%D0%BD%D0%B0%D0%BF%D0%B8%D1%82%D0%BE%D0%BA%29.jpg/500px-%D0%A2%D0%90%D0%9D_%28%D0%BD%D0%B0%D0%BF%D0%B8%D1%82%D0%BE%D0%BA%29.jpg","w":500,"h":511,"by":"Schekinov Alexey Victorovich","lic":"CC BY-SA 4.0","licUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%D0%A2%D0%90%D0%9D_(%D0%BD%D0%B0%D0%BF%D0%B8%D1%82%D0%BE%D0%BA).jpg"}},"ingredient/krupuk":{"t":"ingredient","r":"ingredient/krupuk","n":"Krupuk","c":"#c9b8dd","crumb":[["Ingredients","ingredients"]],"sub":"Ingredient in 6 dishes across 2 cuisines","intro":[["Krupuk, also known as keropok (Malay pronunciation: [/kəˈro.poʔ/]) and kropek (Filipino pronunciation: [/ˈkro.pɛk/]) refers to traditional deep-fried crackers made from starch combined with flavouring ingredients such as prawn, fish or other seafood. The food has long-standing traditions throughout maritime Southeast Asia and neighbouring coastal regions, particularly in Indonesia, Malaysia, the Philippines, southern Thailand, Brunei and Singapore."]],"src":"wikipedia","actions":[{"kind":"globe","hash":"ingredient-krupuk"},{"kind":"wiki","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Krupuk"}],"sections":[{"h":"Where it's cooked with","count":2,"groups":[["Indonesian cuisine","cuisine/indonesian-cuisine",[["Gado-gado","dish/gado-gado"],["Karedok","dish/karedok"],["Ketoprak (dish)","dish/ketoprak-dish"],["Lontong dekem","dish/lontong-dekem"],["Mie caluk","dish/mie-caluk"],["Nasi lengko","dish/nasi-lengko"]]],["Acehnese cuisine","cuisine/acehnese-cuisine",[["Mie caluk","dish/mie-caluk"]]]]},{"h":"Often cooked with","links":[["Cucumber","ingredient/cucumber"],["Peanut sauce","ingredient/peanut-sauce"],["Lontong","ingredient/lontong"],["Chili pepper","ingredient/chili-pepper"],["Mun